  *   <pin> release               the pin as it was before the first command
  *
  * <pin> is the header pin number or the SoC pin's name (GPIOX_21). Only
- * the header's pins are offered, and the I2C pins only while their I2C
- * function isn't selected (I2C A carries the RTC shield). Switching a pin
+ * the header's pins are offered, without pins 3 and 5 (I2C A, the RTC
+ * shield), and the I2C B pins only while I2C B isn't selected. Switching a pin
  * to GPIO clears the mux bits of its alternate functions; the first command
  * on a pin saves its mux, direction, output and pull bits for "release".
  *
+ *   <pin> pwm <0-255>           a PWM output (pins 11 and 19)
+ *
+ * Programs use ioctls instead (headers/private/drivers/meson_gpio.h): an
+ * open file claims pins, and sets, reads and writes them all at once.
+ * The text commands leave claimed pins alone.
+ *
+ * PWM: pin 11 is the EF controller's slot A (E), pin 19 its slot B (F),
+ * counting the 24 MHz crystal: a period of 255 cycles (94.1 kHz), high for
+ * <duty> of them. 0 and 255 are the pin as a GPIO output, low or high (the
+ * controller can't stay at one level). On the ODROID-C2, PWM B and D drive
+ * the core voltage regulators: their controllers (AB, which also has pin
+ * 33's PWM A, and CD) are never touched, nor anything else that could harm
+ * the board. Pins 3 and 5 (I2C A, the RTC shield) aren't offered at all.
+ * The PWM facts are in the port's docs/m27-geekport-facts.md (from Linux
+ * and the S905 datasheet).
+ *
  * Registers (the periphs pin controller at 0xc8834000): the mux at 0x4b0,
  * the GPIO direction (1 = input), output and input words at 0x430, pull
  * (1 = up) at 0x4e8, pull enable at 0x520; per bank a word and a first bit
